   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Southern Africa Development Community (SADC) Labour Migration Policy Framework seeks to promote sound management of intra-regional labour migration for the benefit of both the sending and receiving countries as well as migrant workers. Adoption of SADC&#x2019;s Labour Migration Policy Framework in 2014 was followed by three Labour Migration Action Plans (LMAPs) designed to implement it: the SADC Labour Migration Action Plan 2013-2015, the SADC Labour Migration Action Plan 2016-2019 and the SADC Labour Migration Action Plan 2020-2025. 

The latest SADC LMAP (2020-2025) was approved at the SADC Employment and Labour Sector Ministerial Meeting in Tanzania, March 2020. The following year, in 2021, SADC Ministers of Employment and Labour and Social Partners approved the Terms of Reference of the SADC Technical Committee on Labour Migration, which oversees the implementation of the SADC LMAP. 

The 2021 SADC Employment and Labour Sector Ministerial Meeting also approved the concept note of the SADC Labour Market Observatory (LMO). The LMO is a mechanism for the collection, consolidation, storage and management of labour market information from Member States through a regional repository and web portal. The LMO will pool available national level labour market information including, among other areas, information concerning labour migration and skills. Furthermore, the LMO will be providing a coordination mechanism involving a number of sectors and key stakeholders.

In March 2020, SADC Ministers and Social Partners adopted Guidelines on Portability of Social Security Benefits in SADC. Eswatini, Lesotho, Malawi, South Africa and Zimbabwe volunteered to pilot the implementation of the Guidelines. The Guidelines include provisions for the monitoring of its implementation. The Southern Africa Development Community (SADC) labour migration Action Plan (LMAP) 2020-25 was developed with support from ILO through technical inputs into the LMAP, with financial assistance from the European Union. To support the implementation of the LMAP, a SADC Scoping study and guiding document for the implementation plan of the SADC LMAP (2020-2025) was developed with support from the ILO. The ILO will actively support the  implementation of the LMAP. 

The SADC Technical Committee on Labour Migration was established with technical support from the ILO, including inputs for the Terms of Reference. A progress report on the implementation of the LMAP will be presented at the second meeting of the SADC TCLM in September 2021 highlighting the support in particular from the ILO. 

With the support of the European Union, the ILO has been supporting the establishment of the SADC Labour Market Observatory (LMO) in collaboration with the SADC secretariat.  ILO&#x2019;s support has included the development of the Concept Note and the Terms of Reference of the Governance Structure of the LMO. ILO also undertook stocktaking exercises regarding the availability of data and information in Member States. Finally, ILO is preparing technical tools and training to help implement the LMO such as software for storage of information and indicators.

With the support of the European Union, the ILO supported the adoption of the Guidelines on Portability of Social Security Benefits by undertaking background sectoral studies on the mining and agriculture sector, contributing to the drafting of the Guidelines and facilitating tripartite consultations. The ILO is also providing capacity building and technical support, including development of Action Plans for Implementation of the Guidelines in pilot countries.The ILO is supporting the SADC Secretariat to develop a simplified Version of the Guidelines for Migrants, Employers as well as information and communications materials.</narrative>
   </description>
